There is something different about humans. Other animals may
have memes and a few might even have memetic evolution of a sort
but it's pretty meager, it evolves  no faster and clearly plays
second fiddle to genetic evolution. With humans memetic
evolution is crucial. Without it we'd just be another biped that
never learned how to use stone tools. Upload a man and speed him
up a billion times and he will advance and eventually  and learn
how to augment his intelligence. Upload a population of insects
and they might eventual evolve intelligence but it is by no
means a sure thing. Upload an individual cockroach and all get
is a super fast bug, forever. I don't know exactly where the
threshold is or how many neurons it takes but somewhere between
bugs and humans it's got to be there. 
	      

	      >while the world is certainly a better and more interesting
	      >place since the invention of the transistor, it is by no means  
	      >unimaginable to someone living before 1957. 

I think the transistor was invented in 1948 ,not that it
matters. None of our previous technological advances improved us
they improved our tools , in the singularity we become our tools
and that is fundamentally new. I really don't want to draw a
historic parallel to the singularity because I don't think
anything does it justice but if you put a gun to my head I
suppose I'd say The Cambrian Explosion 570 million years ago.
Life is over 4 billion years old but for 85% of that time nature
could produce nothing better than micro organisms ,then in less
than 10 million years, possibly much less, multi cellular life
appeared and everything changed. You could now find enormous
carnivores like "Anomalocaris" a animal nearly 3 feet in length,
or a well named beast unlike anything alive today called
"Hallucigenia" that looked like a cross between that thing in
the movie "Alien" and a bad LSD trip.
	      
More important than those two oddballs was "Pikaia" the first
chordate, our own phila. In fact, nearly all the 32 phyla that
make up life today, and some that no longer exist, got their
start during this same brief period. Nobody knows why things
happened so suddenly but I think there must have been some bio
chemical problem ( the need for efficient calcification ?) that
was holding back progress, when nature finally solved the
problem through trail and error there was an unprecedented
explosion of creativity and experimentation. Some experiments
worked out ,like the Chordates others led to dead ends like
Hallucigenia.
	      
Other than the origin of life itself this was the most important
time in the history of biology. Some would give that lofty title
to the evolution of Homo Sapiens but I think it's much too soon
to be sure about that, we need more time so we can develop a
historical perspective. It may not be a singularity but it's the
best I could come up with.
  
