Subject: [#94-12-432] PHYS: New model for gravity

From: vincek@intergalact.com (Vince Kerchner) :

>The most recent (12/3/94) issue of "Science News" contains an article
>describing a new modification to General Relativity made by Huseyin Yilmaz
>of Tufts University and Carroll Alley of the University of Maryland.

About Einstein's General Relativity theory...

Einstein's General Relativity actually hasn't been as throughly
tested as it could be.  Einstein built it on the equivalence
principle of inertial and gravitational mass. This principle states
that the ratio of gravitational mass to inertial mass is unity for
all materials, even antimatter.

The equivalence principle has been verified to better than one part
in 10^11 for neutral matter, but has not been fully verified for
*charge* constituents of neutral matter (e.g. electrons, positrons,
etc.).

If the principle of equivalence if found to be violated, it will be a
significant event in the history of physics, because the principle
is the foundation on which the gravitational theories of both Newton
and Einstein rest.

In 1957, Gerhart Luders used his CPT theorem to prove that the
mathematical operations that transform the description of a particle
into a description of an antiparticle leave the laws of physics
intact. But that was mathematics. It is important to test the
Equivalence Principle in a real-world experiment.

A number of research teams have investigated composition-dependent
effects of gravity, with some positive and some negative results. As
you may guess, it is tricky to measure the gravitational effect on
charged particles, since electromagnetic forces are so much greater
than gravitational forces. Nevertheless, some scientists have
developed clever techniques to measure the gravitational
acceleration of antiprotons and electrons. Those experiments are in
progress now. (I wrote a computer simulation to test one aspect of
the Los Alamos antiproton experiment for my thesis a few years
ago.)

Ok, so the General Theory of Relativity hasn't been thoroughly tested
for antimatter, are there other problems with it? Yes, there is a
serious incompatibility between quantum mechanics and the principle
of equivalence that underlie classical theories of gravitation. For
example, according to the equivalence principle, an object's intial
position and velocity determine a definite trajectory for a freely
falling object in a gravitational field. In quantum mechanics,
however, the object's path is indeterminate and probabilistic.

The incompatibilities between quantum physics and gravity have led
many physicists to consider ways to extend general relativity in
order to make gravitation more amenable to quantization. None of the
quantum gravity theories have been shown to be mathematically
consistent, however.

Until now??

Yilmaz and Alley claim that their modified General Relativity 
Theory is compatible with quantum mechanics, so maybe the scientists
should take them seriously.

Is There a Growing Dissatisifaction with the Basic Physics Theories?

In the November/December 1994 issue of The Sciences, several
scientists describe a new "Zero-Point-Field Theory" that apparently
caused quite a stir when they submitted it to Physical Review A. (It
was published.)

The theory turns traditional definitions of mass on its ear. They say
that mass, inertia, and gravity arise from underlying electromagnetic
processes. They say that E=mc^2 is actually a statement about how much
energy is required to give *the appearance* of a certain amount of
mass, rather than about the conversion of one fundamental thing,
energy, into another fundamental thing, mass. So their view is that
the physical universe is made up of massless electric charges immersed
in a vast, energetic, all-pervasive electromagnetic field. It is the
interaction of those charges and the electromagnetic field that
creates the appearance of mass. Their theory can go pretty far at
unifying the forces because the forces and effects are not disparate
entities. Just as mass would arise from the electromagnetic force,
in their theory, so wuold gravity.

I think that a kind of movement is underway in physics to revamp
alot of the old theories, and my response is: "It's about time!!"

Amara

