From: vincek@intergalact.com (Vince Kerchner)
Date: Sun, 11 Dec 1994 00:23:51 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-433] ECON: Book censorship?

I must admit that I was really begging the question on this topic, and I
had a fair idea of the repsonses I would get.

I chose this topic becuase, in the area where I live, books are a commodity
for which there is little geographical, service-spectrum, or price-based
competition.  Bookstores are copious: I can get to several by walking or
driving less than 5 minutes, so there is little reason for choosing one
over another based on location.  Most stock about 90% of the books I
usually want to buy, so there is little reason for choosing one over
another based on range of selection (although there are specialty shops
which do maintain a competetive advantage for about 10% of the topics I am
interested in).  They all sell new books for the same price, so there is
virtually no reason to bargain-shop (I consider the used bookstores a
separate market, as there are competetive differences between them in all
three categories I mention.)

What, then, is the basis for my choosing to patronize one establishment
over another, and why, over time, might I form a preference for a specific
and very non-random subset of all such establishments (which I do)?  If,
every time I wanted to buy a particular book, I knew I could get it at the
same price, with essentially the same probability of availability, at every
establishment, wouldn't I just pick a random vector and head that way until
I encountered the first bookstore, hand over my money, and be done with it?
This is the way I buy gasoline; what's so different about books?

Two possible reasons come to mind:

1.  In addition to simply paying for the product I receive, I implicitly
acknowledge that part of my money is going to capitalize the business I
choose to patronize.  Thus, I become "part of the loop".  While I do not
expect to receive dividends from this capital expenditure in the form of
money, I do form an expectation of my ability to receive additional,
similar, services from the establishment in the future.  In short, I
support the establishment by investing in its ability to remain solvent,
irrespective of the success or failure of its competetors: I become a
"patron".  This is not the way I buy gasoline, groceries, and a variety of
other goods: I really don't care what happens to an individual business;
I'll just go somewhere else if it folds.

2.  The value I receive from the establishment is not entirely contained in
the product I purchase, and, in fact, I may recieve value without making
any purchase at all.  Ambiance, layout, lighting, the music they play,
whether there is a cafe upstairs, the other clientele, etc... all
contribute to this value.  Often, I will go to a bookstore simply for the
entertainment value alone, whether I eventually make a purchase or not.  I
also recieve value from the information content of the inventory, both by
the ability to perform short-term research on a specific topic without
cost, as well as by the ability simply to peruse the current distribution
and availability of titles and their contents before I purchase.

Thus, it seems to me that bookstores are an example of an economic
institution which operates on several different levels, and for which the
governing mechanisms extend far beyond the point-of-sale transaction.  I
rarely go to the supermarket to spend a few hours of quality time in the
frozen-vegetable section, or to catch up on the latest selection of soups
:-).

tburns@mason1.gmu.edu (T. David Burns) sez:

>"Assuming that a bookstore does
>selectively choose what to stock and sell based on content," should I get
>pissed off? I don't see why.

Given the argument outlined above, there is no reason to do so.  I would
have been surprised if you had suggested that I should.  As long as I have
other alternatives, it is my responsibility to exercise them, not the
market's responsibility to cater to them.  If, however, there are no other
alternatives, then perhaps I would have legitimate cause for concern.

nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu (Nancy Lebovitz) sez:

>I'd prefer reserving the word censorship for restricting the information
>flow by violence

Agreed, and I think this is the central theme: the availability of choice,
without coercion.  As long as I am not forced to buy a particular product,
or prevented from buying another by means other than price (or the
willingness of the proprietor), the only tool I have at my disposal to
provide feedback to the market is how I choose to distribute my funds (if
at all).

>Does it make a difference if the seller gives the impression of
>carrying the whole field but really doesn't?

That depends on whether you feel false-advertising is a crime.  In general,
I don't, as long as some kind of minimal guarantee as to the workmanship of
the product enables me to recover my purchase price.  Even in the absence
of such a guarantee, the reputation of the seller is at stake.  I may lose
the purchase price of an individual item, but the seller risks losing that
price multiplied by the number of potential customers if a substantial part
of her business goes to her competetors due to deception.

--Vince

