From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Sun, 11 Dec 1994 01:08:01 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-12-435] ECON: Book censorship?

Tim May has been discussing how we need a new word to sit between
"censorship" (which in its strict definition applies only to acts of the
state) and "editing" (which refers to acceptable content discrimination).
This new word would refer to voluntary content discrimination by
a private sector content provider that is considered to be
ethically unacceptable, reason enough for boycott of the provider
or setting up competition.  (but not reason enough to pass laws 
against the activity).  Such a word might describe nicely what
Vince has encountered, without confounding it with the more
odious, involuntary government censorship.

Bookstores commonly discriminate based on their cultural outlook, 
despite the bottom line, so bookstores are one place where I
do try to "vote with my pocketbook".  The Stanford Bookstore is 
one very large bookstore here in the Bay Area that I find to have a 
slightly Extropian-friendly slant.  (Very good economics and 
technical sections, for example).

BTW, so we can join the boycott, which bookstore specifically?

